        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: stabilize embedded prompt caching across policy and Responses boundaries",
        "body": "Related: #102175\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nLong-lived embedded sessions could lose prompt-cache reuse when the same session crossed room-event delivery, sender-policy, queue, compaction, recovery, or native Responses continuation boundaries.\n\nThe failure was not one cache knob. Turn-specific delivery and authorization changed reusable prompt/tool shape, reconstructed runs could drop authoritative state, and authenticated OpenAI Responses could miss native continuation.\n\n## What Changed\n\n- Keeps the model-visible prompt and ordered tool catalogue stable across owner, non-owner, trusted-internal, unknown-sender, and ambient room-event turns.\n- Resolves one deterministic `ToolAccessPolicy` per turn and emits a trusted full snapshot only when permissions change or model state is uncertain.\n- Denies unavailable `computer`, `cron`, `gateway`, and `nodes` calls with non-retryable `TOOL_ACCESS_DENIED` before hooks, implementations, or side effects.\n- Carries immutable policy through loopback MCP grants; request headers cannot widen authority, and current-main capability/revocation checks remain intact.\n- Keeps trusted-internal turns non-owner (`senderIsOwner: false`): policy can grant only the four protected core tools without exposing owner-only plugin tools.\n- Separates actual reply delivery from the session-stable prompt-facing delivery mode, including `message_tool_only` room events.\n- Preserves an explicit forced `message` tool on automatic Codex turns while keeping `disableMessageTool` as the hard veto.\n- Propagates policy, owner identity, delivery modes, and forced snapshots through direct, queued, memory, preflight, manual, timeout, overflow, and after-turn compaction paths.\n- Acknowledges policy persistence at provider-native durable boundaries for OpenClaw, Codex, and Copilot, with generation checks preventing stale acknowledgements from clearing newer state.\n- Preserves native Codex supervision settings while appending trusted turn guidance as a developer history item before the user turn; no model, effort, personality, or collaboration-mode override is introduced.\n- Routes built-in authenticated GPT-5.5 and GPT-5.6 Responses streams through native `previous_response_id` continuation with delta input. Custom streams keep their existing routing.\n\nThe earlier experimental post-history developer-message split is not included; that request shape produced HTTP 400 responses on ChatGPT/Codex Responses. Native continuation is fixed through stream classification instead.\n\n## Security And Compatibility\n\n- Tool visibility is not authority. Every protected call is checked again against the immutable current-turn policy immediately before execution.\n- Runtime-owned `cron`, `heartbeat`, and `memory` turns are distinct from unknown and explicit non-owner senders. Ambient room-event policy remains fail-closed.\n- The model-facing tool-name hash omits turn-specific owner state; the runtime grant retains the effective decision.\n- No persistent storage schema or migration is introduced.\n- No private sender, channel, account, host, endpoint, credential, or raw trace identifiers are included.\n\n## Evidence\n\nThe original same-session reproduction showed the model-visible inventory flipping between 44 and 47 tools, matching prompt/tool digest changes, and cached prefixes collapsing to roughly 2k-10k tokens or ceilinging around 37k-40k.\n\nNumeric-only usage records from a long-lived room session after the stable-shape change show the cached prefix growing from 104,960 to 157,184 tokens. Its final four consecutive provider turns reported `cacheRead` 155,136 / 155,136 / 156,160 / 157,184 with uncached input 1,193 / 1,836 / 2,097 / 1,324, or 98.7-99.2% prefix reuse. Message text, sender/channel/session IDs, hosts, and credentials were excluded.\n\nThe exact candidate head was then exercised through isolated, non-delivered live-provider sessions:\n\n- GPT-5.5: cold `input=41,045 cacheRead=0`; warm turns `input=127 cacheRead=40,960` and `input=169 cacheRead=40,960` (99.6-99.7% reuse).\n- GPT-5.6 Sol: cold `input=41,048 cacheRead=0`; warm turns `input=642 cacheRead=40,448` and `input=684 cacheRead=40,448` (98.3-98.4% reuse).\n- The system-prompt hash stayed constant within each model, the ordered catalogue remained 48 tools, and the aggregate ordered tool name/summary/schema digest was identical across warm turns and both models: `74ba8ac01af35175aed175bc359d3930a814c8392423ab36030ad783b56ba0ef`.\n\nAn exact-head focused proof run selected 14 assertions across 18 project suites with zero failures. It verifies room events remain contextual and `message_tool_only`, unauthorized non-owner and ambient calls return non-retryable `TOOL_ACCESS_DENIED` before hooks or implementation, trusted-internal calls execute, and GPT-5.5/GPT-5.6 send `previous_response_id` with delta-only input across unchanged and changed policy snapshots.\n\nThe exact candidate head is now built and running on the reporter's local gateway. The LaunchAgent uses the repo-local `dist/index.js`, runtime and RPC probes are healthy, the loopback dashboard returns HTTP 200, and `dist/build-info.json` reports the exact commit below.\n\n## Exact-Head Validation\n\nCandidate: `5258d4f53e19dde32262e29a8563cdcf60ec3c3e`\n\nUpstream included in branch and used for merge-tree proof: `350bac4e0e2b73ad678f962cb0d28956e10f357c`\n\n- merge-tree check against the latest fetched upstream is clean;\n- pre-refresh broad validation passed 2,446 changed-file tests across unit-fast, unit, gateway, auto-reply, agents, Codex, and extension shards, including all 86 loopback MCP tests in their owning gateway-server shard;\n- exact-head proof passed 14 selected assertions across 18 suites with zero failures;\n- exact-head Codex lifecycle, binding, and login-profile compatibility passed 215 tests;\n- exact-head Codex automatic-turn force-message regression passed all 60 tests in its owning shard;\n- focused CLI preparation tests confirm trusted-internal policy is preserved while owner identity remains false;\n- prior focused suites passed 173 compaction/overflow, 98 harness-selection, and 15 manual-compaction propagation tests;\n- prompt snapshots are current;\n- `pnpm tsgo:prod` passed;\n- `pnpm check:test-types` passed;\n- `pnpm lint:core` and `pnpm lint:extensions` passed;\n- `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD` passed;\n- `pnpm build` passed;\n- exact local install: OpenClaw `2026.7.2`, LaunchAgent loaded, runtime active, connectivity probe OK, HTTP 200, build-info commit matches candidate, Discord probe OK, and WhatsApp linked/healthy.\n\nThe merge also resolves current-main changes to Codex native supervision, ring-zero tool composition, model-lock compaction, Computer Use health probes, and generated prompt snapshots without dropping either side's behavior.\n\nDisclaimer: This PR was filed and updated autonomously by Codex on behalf of the reporter. Private user, channel, host, credential, and full trace identifiers were removed.\n",
